<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xining</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Qingtang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xining is the capital of the Chinese province of Qinghai, on the Huangshui river at the north-eastern edge of the Tibetan plateau. In the 11th century, as Qingtang, it was the court of the Tibetan ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gusiluo">Gusiluo</a>. Under the Ming and Qing dynasties it was a garrison and trading town between the Chinese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, and the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> monastery of Kumbum lies nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xining.jpg" alt="Xining" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyscrapers_in_Chengzhong_District,_Xining_(53727167629)_-_2024.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Xining lies in the valley of the Huangshui River, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, at an average altitude of about 2,200 metres on the eastern edge of the Qinghai–Tibet Plateau. It is about 200 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanzhou">Lanzhou</a> and stood on the traditional main trade route from northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> into Tibet and the Qaidam Basin. It was a commercial hub on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> caravan route, handling especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, wool and salt in ancient times, as part of the Northern Silk Road, whose use grew in the 1st century BCE after Han efforts to control it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> a county called Linqiang was set up to control the local Qiang tribesmen. It was a frontier county again under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a> and Tang, and in the 7th and early 8th centuries a centre of constant warfare with the Tuyuhun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> overran it in 763, and the Chinese knew it under Tibetan control as Qingtang. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> recovered it in 1104 and named it Xining, meaning peace in the west; it has been the seat of a prefecture or superior prefecture under that name since then.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsongkhapa">Tsongkhapa</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school, was born in the region, and Kumbum Monastery was founded at his birthplace in the late 16th century, making Xining an important centre for the Gelug school. A magnitude 7.6 earthquake on 22 May 1927 killed over 40,000 people. Xining stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> until 1928, when it became capital of the new province of Qinghai. Japanese aircraft bombed it in 1941, and the Salar general Han Youwen directed its defence. It received municipal status in 1945. Under Governor Ma Bufang it was industrialised and modernised, and the rail link to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanzhou">Lanzhou</a>, completed in 1959, spurred industry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Xining is the capital and most populous city of Qinghai and the largest city on the Tibetan Plateau, with about 2.5 million inhabitants at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Only about four of its roughly 37 nationalities are numerically significant: the Han, Hui, Monguor or Tu, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a>. Kumbum Monastery, one of six leading monasteries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school, lies to the south-west, and the Dongguan Mosque, over 600 years old, is one of the best known in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. A railway reaches <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>, opened in 2006, and the city has a cool summer climate, which has earned it the name Summer Resort Capital of China.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Xining” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the 18th century it was nominally under Qing supervision from <b>Xining</b>, while maintaining close religious ties with Lhasa.</blockquote><a href="/state/nangchen">Nangchen</a></li><li><blockquote>After years as a figurehead of local strongmen he established his own court at <b>Qingtang</b> (modern Xining) around 1032.</blockquote><a href="/state/tsongkha">Tsongkha</a></li></ul></section>
